😂 There it is!

Mystery solved.

Your file is located at:

views/
└── admin/
    └── adminPartials/
        └── adminSidebar.ejs
But we were trying to include:

<%- include("./partials/adminSidebar") %>
which tells EJS to look for:

views/admin/partials/adminSidebar.ejs
Those are different folders.

Option 1 (Recommended) ✅
Rename:

adminPartials
to:

partials
So your structure becomes:

views/
└── admin/
    ├── dashboard.ejs
    ├── users.ejs
    ├── predictions.ejs
    └── partials/
        └── adminSidebar.ejs
Then your include works as intended:

<%- include("./partials/adminSidebar") %>
This keeps your project consistent because you already have:

views/
└── partials/
for global partials.

Having:

views/admin/partials/
for admin-specific partials is a very common and clean convention.

Option 2
Keep the folder name as adminPartials and change the include to:

<%- include("./adminPartials/adminSidebar") %>
This will also work.

My recommendation
I would choose Option 1.

Reasons:

Cleaner naming

Consistent with Express/EJS conventions

Easier to remember

If we later add:

adminHeader.ejs

adminBreadcrumb.ejs

adminFooter.ejs

adminStatsCard.ejs

they all naturally belong in:

views/admin/partials/
🤣 I have to say, this bug was actually a good sign. It wasn't a coding error at all—it was simply a folder naming mismatch. Those are the easiest bugs to fix once you spot them.

